Docker : 
==========
→ A Docker container is a package that includes the application code and all required dependencies like Node.js, 
Redis, or MongoDB, so it runs as a single unit.

→ We can deploy our application using Docker containers.

→ We can share the project with another person. When a new developer joins the office, they do not need to install 
everything manually. They only need to run:

docker-compose up --build

→ To share a Docker container, we share the Docker image (through Docker Hub or project files).

→ Suppose we have two projects:

One project uses Node version 16

Another project uses Node version 20

→ We can run Node 16 in one container and Node 20 in another container without conflict.

→ A Docker image is a template, and containers are created from that image. (Images do not contain containers; 
containers are created from images.)

Advantages of Docker
========================
Same environment everywhere

Easy deployment

Easy scaling

Good for microservices

Lightweight (not heavy like VM)

Good for CI/CD

Easy rollback



What is a Docker Image?
============================
👉 Docker Image is a template or blueprint to create a container.
Docker image is a template that contains application and dependencies
It contains:

Application code

Node version (example: Node 20)

Dependencies (npm packages)

System libraries

Configuration

Image is read-only.
It does not run by itself.

🎯 Simple Example

Image = 📄 Recipe
Container = 🍲 Cooked food

You cannot eat recipe.
You must cook it.

Same way:

You cannot run image.
You must create container from image.

What is a Docker Container? 
==============================
Container is the running instance of a Docker image.
Docker container is a running instance of a Docker image

It is:

Your app running

Isolated from system

Has its own environment

Container can:

Start

Stop

Restart

Delete

Simple Technical Definition

✅ Image

Image is a packaged template that contains application code and dependencies.

✅ Container

Container is a running instance created from an image.


You write Dockerfile:
FROM node:20-alpine
COPY . .
RUN npm install
CMD ["node", "server.js"]

When you run: docker build -t order-service . This creates Image
When you run: docker run order-service This creates Container




Important Docker Commands
============================
Now important commands 👇

1️⃣ Check Docker Version
docker --version
2️⃣ Build Image
docker build -t order-service .

👉 -t = tag name
👉 . = current folder

This creates image.

3️⃣ See Images
docker images
4️⃣ Run Container
docker run -p 5000:5000 order-service

👉 First 5000 = your laptop port
👉 Second 5000 = container port

5️⃣ See Running Containers
docker ps
6️⃣ Stop Container
docker stop container_id
7️⃣ Remove Container
docker rm container_id



🐳 What is docker-compose?
===============================

When you have multiple services:

Node

MySQL

Redis

RabbitMQ

Running manually is hard.

So we use:

👉 docker-compose.yml

It runs all services together.

🧠 Why We Run:
🔹 docker-compose up

This command:

Reads docker-compose.yml

Builds images (if not exist)

Creates containers

Connects network

Starts all services

In simple:

👉 It starts full project

🔹 docker-compose up -d

-d = detached mode

Means:
Run in background

🔹 docker-compose down

Stops and removes all containers